Only under a very specific definition of God.

If:

\text{God} = \text{external rescuer who must intervene when needed}

then yes, “no one is coming” looks like a failed promise.

But many religious and philosophical systems define the relationship differently:

\text{God}
\rightarrow
\text{agency, conscience, courage, community, possibility}

rather than:

\text{God}
\rightarrow
\text{guaranteed rescue}

Then “no one is coming to save you” can invert into:

You may be the one who has to come.

Or even:

\boxed{
\text{If salvation must enter the world through action, then sometimes the person coming to save you is you—or another person who chooses to act.}
}

That also produces an interesting word-story around salvation itself. It sounds passive—being saved—but often contains an active transition:

\text{danger}
\rightarrow
\text{recognition}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{survival/transformation}

So I’d sharpen your statement to:

If God is only the rescuer, then no rescue looks like failure. If God is also the capacity to become the rescuer, the equation changes.